For instance, consider the following example:

```java
interface Animal {
    void makeSound();
}

class Dog implements Animal {
    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Woof!");
    }
}

class Cat implements Animal {
    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Meow!");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal myDog = new Dog();
        Animal myCat = new Cat();

        myDog.makeSound(); // Outputs: Woof!
        myCat.makeSound();  // Outputs: Meow!
    }
}
```

In this example, `Animal` is the superclass, and `Dog` and `Cat` are subclasses. The `makeSound` method is overridden in each subclass, allowing us to call the same method name (`makeSound`) but get different behaviors based on the actual object type at runtime.

